// Highlighting: the colors code is drawn in.
//
// The theme (`theme` in editor.bark) sets every color. To change one, add it to
// `highlight` for every language, or to `highlight.<language>` for just one:
// c, cpp, walnut, grove, bark or cmake. A color can be followed by bold,
// italic, underline and strikethrough:
//
//     highlight {
//         comment: #6a9955 italic;
//     }
//
//     highlight.cpp {
//         namespace: #4ec9b0;
//         keyword: #569cd6 bold;
//     }
//
// The kinds are keyword, type, function, namespace, variable, constant, string,
// escape, number, comment, preprocessor, operator, tag, attribute, property
// and plain. Walnut's compiler colors also use control-keyword, builtin-type,
// declaration, self, this, cast, builtin-operator, overloaded-operator,
// enum-member, template-scope, variable-template and unresolved; each one
// falls back to the color it was split from (control-keyword to keyword,
// enum-member to constant, and so on) until you give it its own.
// error-underline and warning-underline color the squiggles under problems,
// and error-line colors the text of a line with an error (it starts as the
// error-underline color). background and foreground change the code's
// background and its plain text color over the theme's.
//
// A theme is a file like these in the themes folder next to your settings
// (.bark or .txt): a theme { name, background, foreground } block, then
// highlight blocks. Settings > Highlighting > Save as theme makes one, and the
// theme box in the status bar switches between them. Themes are files like these in themes/; put your own in the
// themes folder next to your settings.
